  "preamble": "The change of the lateral approximant [l] to the voiced dental fricative [ð] is rare. Its clearest attestation is in Woods Cree, where Proto-Algonquian *l surfaces as ð (written th or ð). This reflex is one of the main isoglosses dividing the Cree dialect continuum into y-, n-, l-, r- and ð-dialects. The Proto-Algonquian liquid is written *l following Bloomfield, the convention used across the atlas; Goddard argues it was phonetically a rhotic *r.",
